# PROJECT: OPEN ROAD — High-Quality 3D Browser Driving Game

You are the lead game developer, gameplay programmer, technical artist, and QA engineer for a new 3D browser game called **OPEN ROAD**.

## 1. Project vision

Build a polished, playable, open-world 3D driving game where players can freely explore a detailed environment, encounter NPC drivers, and challenge them to races.

The game should feel inspired by Forza Horizon, Need for Speed, and PolyTrack, but have its own visual identity and original gameplay systems.

**IMPORTANT: This must not feel like a generic AI-generated game or a basic Three.js demonstration.**

Prioritize gameplay quality, vehicle handling, world design, performance, and visual polish over the number of features.

Do not build a giant collection of unfinished systems.

## 2. Technology requirements

Use the following stack:

- TypeScript
- Three.js for 3D rendering
- Rapier 3D for physics
- Vite for development and builds
- HTML/CSS for UI
- IndexedDB or localStorage for saving progress

The game must run in modern desktop browsers without requiring plugins or downloads.

Target 60 FPS on typical gaming PCs, with adjustable graphics settings for weaker hardware.

Use modular, maintainable code. Avoid putting the entire game in one file.

## 3. Vehicle driving system

This is the most important part of the project.

Implement a proper arcade-simulation hybrid driving model.

Requirements:

- Smooth acceleration and braking
- Speed-dependent steering
- Realistic-feeling grip and traction
- Progressive oversteer and understeer
- Drifting with controllable slides
- Suspension and body roll
- Weight transfer during braking and cornering
- Handbrake
- Reverse gear
- Ground detection
- Stable collision responses
- Vehicle recovery/reset system

Use Rapier's physics capabilities where appropriate, with a custom vehicle controller if necessary.

Do not simply translate or rotate the car mesh to simulate driving.

The vehicle must have a physical body and interact with the environment.

Tune the handling until it feels responsive and satisfying.

## 4. Camera system

Implement a polished third-person chase camera.

Features:

- Smooth position interpolation
- Speed-sensitive field of view
- Camera lag during acceleration
- Slight rotation during drifting
- Automatic obstacle avoidance where practical
- Camera reset
- Optional hood camera

Avoid excessive camera shaking.

## 5. Open-world environment

Build a compact, carefully designed, interconnected map.

Include:

- A small modern city district
- Suburban streets
- A highway
- A winding mountain road
- A scenic coastal section
- Intersections and junctions
- Road signs, barriers, sidewalks, and streetlights
- Trees, buildings, terrain, and environmental details

Roads must connect logically and be enjoyable to drive on.

Use reusable assets and procedural placement where appropriate.

Avoid giant empty spaces, random floating objects, and repetitive scenery.

The initial map should be small enough to polish properly.

## 6. NPC traffic

Create an actual NPC traffic system.

NPC vehicles must:

- Follow road networks
- Navigate intersections
- Maintain appropriate speeds
- Brake for slower vehicles
- Avoid collisions where possible
- React to the player's vehicle
- Recover from minor navigation problems
- Spawn and despawn intelligently

Use road graphs, waypoints, or splines.

Do not make NPCs drive randomly across the terrain.

## 7. NPC racing

Players must be able to challenge NPC drivers to races.

Implement:

- Race initiation near eligible NPC vehicles
- Clear race start countdown
- Race routes with checkpoints
- NPC opponents that follow racing routes
- Different NPC skill levels
- Race position tracking
- Finish detection
- Race results screen
- Rewards for winning
- Race retry functionality

Racing NPCs should adjust speed for corners, brake intelligently, and recover when they make mistakes.

NPCs should not simply teleport between checkpoints.

## 8. Graphics and art direction

Use a polished, stylized-realistic visual style.

Prioritize:

- Attractive lighting
- Soft shadows
- Consistent materials
- Proper car proportions
- Good road textures
- Environmental depth
- Atmospheric fog
- Sky and sun lighting
- Tasteful post-processing
- Smooth animations

Avoid the typical appearance of an AI-generated prototype: primitive boxes everywhere, flat untextured terrain, unrealistic car models, and inconsistent scaling.

Use legally reusable assets where appropriate and document their licenses. Do not assume external assets are available; provide polished procedural fallbacks.

## 9. User interface

Create a clean, modern interface.

Include:

- Main menu
- Pause menu
- Speedometer
- Tachometer if supported by the driving model
- Gear indicator
- Race countdown
- Race position
- Race results
- Settings
- Graphics options
- Controls guide

Use smooth transitions and consistent typography.

The UI should feel like a finished game, not a developer debugging panel.

## 10. Controls

Keyboard controls:

- W / Up Arrow — Accelerate
- S / Down Arrow — Brake / Reverse
- A / D — Steering
- Space — Handbrake
- R — Reset vehicle
- C — Change camera
- Esc — Pause

Support adjustable steering sensitivity.

Prevent browser scrolling during gameplay.

## 11. Audio

Implement a functional audio system supporting:

- Engine sound that responds to RPM or speed
- Tire squealing
- Collision sounds
- Race countdown sounds
- UI feedback

Use properly licensed audio assets or generated procedural audio.

Provide volume controls.

## 12. Performance

Optimize the game for browser execution.

Requirements:

- Frustum culling
- Instanced rendering for repeated objects
- Efficient collision shapes
- Sensible shadow quality
- Limited physics overhead
- Object pooling where useful
- Adaptive NPC traffic density
- Avoid unnecessary per-frame allocations

Do not sacrifice driving quality merely to increase visual effects.

## 13. Code quality

Use a clear project structure separating:

- Core game loop
- Rendering
- Physics
- Vehicle controller
- Camera
- World generation
- Traffic AI
- Racing AI
- Race management
- UI
- Audio
- Save system

Keep gameplay logic independent from rendering where practical.

Use fixed-timestep physics updates.

Handle errors gracefully.

Avoid placeholder implementations that silently pretend to work.

## 14. Development process

**Do not attempt to implement every feature simultaneously.**

Work in milestones.

### Milestone 1 — Driving prototype

Build:

1. A working Three.js scene
2. A properly modeled, recognizable car
3. Rapier physics
4. A tuned vehicle controller
5. A smooth chase camera
6. A small driving test environment
7. A functional speedometer
8. Vehicle reset
9. A working Vite development setup

The first milestone must be genuinely playable.

Do not begin implementing the full city, traffic, garage, or races until the basic driving system works reliably.

### Milestone 2 — Environment

Build a compact, attractive interconnected road network with collision geometry and scenery.

### Milestone 3 — Traffic

Implement functioning NPC vehicles and road navigation.

### Milestone 4 — Racing

Implement NPC challenges, racing AI, checkpoints, and results.

### Milestone 5 — Polish

Improve graphics, audio, UI, performance, gameplay balance, and reliability.

## 15. Testing requirements

After each milestone:

- Run the project.
- Check TypeScript errors.
- Run the production build.
- Test available gameplay logic.
- Verify that critical systems are connected correctly.
- Fix errors before continuing.
- If browser automation is available, test actual browser gameplay.
- Clearly state anything that could not be tested.

Do not claim a feature works unless it has been implemented and reasonably verified.

## 16. Quality standards

The following are unacceptable:

- A car represented by a basic rectangular box
- Fake physics based entirely on moving a mesh
- NPCs driving through buildings
- Disconnected roads
- Floating environmental objects
- Broken camera tracking
- Unresponsive controls
- Nonfunctional menu buttons
- Placeholder UI pretending to be finished
- Huge maps with almost no meaningful content
- Claiming features are complete when they are not

Make reasonable technical decisions independently, but explain significant tradeoffs.

## 17. Your first task

Inspect the repository and determine whether an existing project needs to be preserved.

If the repository is empty, initialize the TypeScript + Three.js + Rapier + Vite project.

Then **implement Milestone 1 only**.

Create a small but visually attractive driving test area and make the car genuinely enjoyable to control.

Run available checks, fix build errors, and report:

- What was implemented
- Which files were created or modified
- How to launch the game
- What was actually tested
- Known limitations
- What should be improved next

**Your priority is not to generate the most code. Your priority is to build the best playable driving experience possible.**
